# ShearQuery — automated YouTube Shorts, once daily at 9am.
#
# INSTALL:
#   crontab scripts/shorts/shorts.cron
# VERIFY:
#   crontab -l
# STOP:
#   crontab -r
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PATH IS SET EXPLICITLY, and this line is the one that decides whether any of
# this works. cron runs with a minimal environment — it does not read .zshrc,
# so it never sees nvm, so `node` is not on its PATH. A job that runs perfectly
# in a shell and does nothing on a schedule is almost always this.
#
# TIMES ARE SYSTEM LOCAL. Currently EDT. The card data is Central; if this
# machine's timezone changes, these fire at a different Central hour than
# intended. cron has no timezone of its own to pin.
#
# THE MACHINE MUST BE AWAKE. A sleeping or powered-off Mac runs no cron and
# does not catch up afterwards — the slot is simply missed, silently. If this
# needs to be reliable, it belongs on a server or a GitHub Action, not a laptop.
#
# WHAT IT DOES EACH RUN: picks the next unpublished card, renders a 9-second
# 1080x1920 Short with the music bed, and publishes it PUBLIC. Guards live in
# run_scheduled.js — a 1/day cap, no repeats, and a hard stop when the card
# pool is exhausted rather than looping.
#
# ONCE A DAY, NOT TWICE, because cadence is limited by supply. Adding a second
# slot here without deepening the card pool just exhausts it twice as fast —
# change MAX_PER_DAY in run_scheduled.js as well, or the second run will simply
# hit the daily cap and log a skip.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

PATH=/Users/lamontevans/.nvm/versions/node/v23.11.0/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin

0 9 * * * cd /Users/lamontevans/Desktop/AI_Blockchain_Enterprise_Services && node scripts/shorts/run_scheduled.js >> "reference/Podcast Visuals/Shorts/_cron.log" 2>&1
